## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical understanding of a full retrieval pipeline, chunking strategy, and vector stores in a beginner-friendly way applicable to large-prod settings (Lyft/Discord/Adobe).

## Key Concepts

- Token-based chunking and overlap
- Local embeddings vs cloud APIs
- Vector databases and cosine similarity
- Data freshness and refresh strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal in-memory demo (no real model)
const chunks = [
  { text: 'chunk one', vec: [0.1,0.2,0.3] },
  { text: 'chunk two', vec: [0.0,0.2,0.4] }
];
function sim(a,b){ let dot = a.reduce((s,x,i)=> s + x*b[i],0); let na=Math.hypot(...a); let nb=Math.hypot(...b); return dot/(na*nb); }
const q=[0.08,0.18,0.28];
const top = chunks.map((c,i)=>({i, s: sim(c.vec,q)})).sort((A,B)=> B.s-A.s)[0];
console.log(top);
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle chunking for languages with complex morphology?
- How would you test for model drift in embeddings over time?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a practical, end-to-end RAG pipeline with real-world constraints: chunking, embedding stability, vector DB tenancy, latency targets, and robust failover. It also probes how to evaluate recall/latency at scale across multiple tenants.

## Key Concepts

- Chunking strategy: 512-token blocks aligned to maintain context.
- Embeddings: stable models (SBERT or OpenAI Ada) with versioned updates.
- Vector DB tenancy: per-repo namespaces and strict access controls.
- Freshness: index aging, re-embedding on document updates.
- Latency: retrieval latency targets (p95/p50) and caching.
- Failover: lexical BM25 fallback and provenance surface.

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: split text into 512-token chunks using a simple tokenizer (word-based placeholder)
function chunkDoc(text) {
  const tokens = text.split(/\s+/);
  const chunkSize = 512;
  const chunks = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < tokens.length; i += chunkSize) {
    chunks.push(tokens.slice(i, i + chunkSize).join(' '));
  }
  return chunks;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure drift in embeddings over time and trigger re-embedding schedules?
- What tenancy/ACL considerations would you enforce for cross-repo queries?
- How would you structure A/B tests for recall vs latency trade-offs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests system design skills for production AI systems, understanding of trade-offs between accuracy and latency, and knowledge of evaluation metrics in real-world scenarios.

## Expected Answer
Strong candidates will discuss: 1) Multi-stage evaluation architecture, 2) Confidence threshold tuning, 3) Fallback strategies, 4) Monitoring and alerting, 5) Cost optimization through early filtering.

## Code Example
```typescript
class HallucinationDetector {
  async evaluate(response: string, context: string) {
    const semanticScore = await this.semanticCheck(response, context);
    if (semanticScore > 0.9) return { confident: true, score: semanticScore };

    const factualScore = await this.factualVerification(response);
    const ensembleScore = this.ensembleWeighting(semanticScore, factualScore);

    return {
      confident: ensembleScore > 0.85,
      score: ensembleScore,
      needsReview: ensembleScore < 0.7
    };
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle edge cases where the knowledge graph is incomplete?
- What metrics would you track to optimize the confidence thresholds?
- How would you design A/B testing for new detection algorithms?

